<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Plassey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Plassey was fought on 23 June 1757 near Palashi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, where a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> defeated the much larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, Nawab of Bengal. The outcome was decided largely by the defection of the nawab's commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, whom the Company then made nawab. The victory gave the Company control of Bengal's revenues and is seen as the beginning of British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey.jpg" alt="Battle of Plassey" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Hayman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lord_Clive_meeting_with_Mir_Jafar_after_the_Battle_of_Plassey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…War (1756–1763), concluded by the Treaty of Paris, stripped France of Canada and its lands in North America east of the Mississippi, confirmed British dominance in India after <b>Plassey</b> (1757), and laid the foundations of the global empire that would reach its zenith in the century after Waterloo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the mid-eighteenth century the East India Company had conquered most of the subcontinent, from Bengal after the <b>Battle of Plassey</b> in 1757 to the Punjab, annexed from the Sikh Empire in 1849.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) brought Robert Clive’s victory at the <b>Battle of Plassey</b> in 1757, the conquest of Quebec after James Wolfe’s victory on the Plains of Abraham in 1759 and naval victories at Lagos and Quiberon Bay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The rising power of the English East India Company, confirmed by its victories at <b>Plassey</b> in 1757 and Buxar in 1764, steadily eroded Mughal sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Plassey</b> in 1757 and the grant of the diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha in 1765, the Bengal Presidency became a territorial government; from 1773 its governor was also Governor-General over the other presidencies.</blockquote><a href="/state/bengal-presidency">Bengal Presidency</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert Clive's victory at <b>Plassey</b> in 1757 over the Nawabate of Bengal made the Company the power behind the nawabs, and the Battle of Buxar in 1764 led to the grant of the diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha by the Mughal emperor in 1765.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India</a></li></ul></section>
